Scope and conclusion
--------------------
The two supported extlinux paths independently assigned preferred/default and
per-config boot_order values. Sorting could put boot/extlinux/extlinux.conf
ahead of extlinux/extlinux.conf because of reverse label ID or path ties.
Assign one boot_order sequence over the concatenated usable entries after scanning
both paths. Preserve each config's preferred flag and the existing global
role/preference/disk/partition comparator. Surviving defaults come first in
config-source order, followed by non-defaults in source/declaration order.

An explicit config-priority field was considered but is unnecessary: the existing
ordered scan plus one ordinal encodes the same extlinux ordering without adding a
field and initialization to every BLS/BootEntry constructor. No parser, DTB search,
partition-discovery, or hardware changes are included.

